0242: Draw the game frame in the present pass instead of through a resample target

The present path resampled the game frame into its own texture, then blitted that texture into the
swap chain image one texel per pixel: `resample_present_source` (lib/webgpu/gpu.cpp) rendered a
full-window pass into `g_resampledFrameBuffer` sized to the present viewport, and the present pass
(lib/aurora.cpp) drew that texture with the copy pipeline over the same rectangle. Both rectangles
are integral and the same size (`calculate_present_viewport` returns whole pixels, `viewport_extent`
rounds the same float back), so the blit sampled every texel at its centre and wrote it unchanged: at
the user's window, 7.06 Mpix and 28.2 MB each of read and write per frame, for a pass whose only
product was the intermediate. With the CRT chain the resample is already skipped (postfx draws the
game frame itself, gpu.cpp `postfx::prepare`); with the default `crt.enabled = false`
(src/port/settings.h) every frame pays it.

The present pass samples `present_source()` with the resampler pipeline
(`webgpu::draw_present_source`): the same pipeline, sampler, uniforms and UVs the resample pass used,
so the pixels written are the ones the resample pass produced. The area sampler derives its source
box from the fragment's position in the target, and the target is the whole window rather than a
viewport-sized texture, so the shader subtracts the viewport origin (new `frame_left`/`frame_top`
uniforms). The removed second sampling was a 1:1 texel fetch (fp32 coordinate error ~1e-7 against
the 0.002 step needed to change a byte). The RmlUi full-screen and CRT paths are untouched.

